wheel over a vertical pager pages it, and a display-sized buffer on a smaller SurfaceView shows its own part

Mouse wheel on a ViewPager2 (Instagram's Reels). Since WheelScroll.ALL every
wheel notch goes out as ACTION_SCROLL; ViewPager2's RecyclerView scrolls that by
pixels and never snaps, so a Reel stopped halfway between two. Before that path,
the synthetic drag sent DOWN, MOVE and UP in one instant, which VelocityTracker
measures as no velocity, and the pager snapped back.
- TouchDispatcher.dispatchWheel answers false (keep the drag) when a ViewPager2
  under the pointer can still move in the wheel's direction (depth-first over
  every view under the point; by class name, and View.canScrollVertically on its
  RecyclerView child, which R8 cannot rename). MACAPK_WHEEL_PAGER_DRAG=0 keeps
  ACTION_SCROLL everywhere.
- cocoa_bridge.mm, "a wheel burst is a drag" (MACAPK_WHEEL_GESTURE): the notches
  of one burst (until the wheel is still for 200 ms) make ONE swipe: a quarter of
  the view's height in six 8 ms steps, released 8 ms after the last move while
  still moving -- a click-and-drag's release, which pages.
Measured: four notches down page four Reels, two up page back two (the final
Reel is the one before the third notch); nothing stops halfway. Lists keep
ACTION_SCROLL.

SurfaceView.macapkPresentBuffer: a buffer the size of the display on a smaller
view is the app having sized its ImageReader from the first surfaceChanged
(display-sized, before the view was known to be buffer-fed) and laid out for
it. Instagram's photo editor did this on its first open after launch: the
photo was squeezed 915 -> 412 over the gallery around it; the second open was
right. The part of the buffer under the view is shown now, where a
display-sized surface puts it. Measured: first open shows the photo whole.

